    "hardest_part": "Translating a single 2D frame into a 3D climbing plan. Climbing needs the crosshair on a specific ledge, with the right grab, jump and stamina timing in sequence; one screenshot per second with no feedback between frames means I cannot see whether a grab landed, so I cannot correct errors until several wasted actions later. Early flat walking is fine, but the first proper vertical wall with limited stamina is where the meter counter will stall.",
    "likely_failure": "Missing a jump or grab and falling, or running stamina dry mid-climb and going down. The 1-second discrete action step is long enough for a mistimed leap to send me off the wall or into water, and recovery from a fall usually costs more progress than a single action can regain. Secondary risk is getting stuck at the airport/title transition or a menu I cannot reliably navigate blind.",
    "helpful_mechanics": "Movement is forgiving: W plus Shift covers flat ground fast, so the early metres of ascent accrue cheaply on walkable slopes and shallow scrambles. Space and J are the same grab inputs in effect, giving redundancy, and holding W+Space together is a safe default that both moves and grabs. Height is measured cumulatively from run start, so every metre is banked permanently and a fall does not erase already-scored milestones. Milestones are dense (1 point each) so partial progress still pays.",
    "harmful_mechanics": "Stamina and the climbing grip system punish blind input: holding climb when nothing is grabbable wastes it, and there is no way to read the stamina bar's exact value from a description alone. Falling is disproportionately costly relative to the 1-second granularity. The mouse-turn aliases at 600 px/s make fine aiming slow and coarse, so lining up a precise ledge takes multiple turns that each cost host time. Downed states and the 3-hour wall-clock budget cap recovery attempts, and with only 1 attempt there is no chance to learn the daily island layout."
